<!-- ==================== PAGE 3 ==================== -->
<div style="margin-bottom: 25px;">
  <div style="flex: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column;">
    <div class="header-row">
      <span>B.Tech DSCPP-III-2026-T101</span>
      <span>PHASE-III PROJECT PROGRESS</span>
    </div>

    <div class="banner">PHASE-III PROJECT PROGRESS DESCRIPTION (35 pts)</div>

    <div class="section-title">Project Abstract (2 pts)</div>
    <div class="instruction-sub">(Brief restatement of your project’s main goal. Max 300 words).</div>
    <div class="content-box">
      <p>All four of us started FundNexus because of something that has always bothered us about online charity portals: once you hit 'Donate', your money basically vanishes into a black box. Platforms raise lakhs of rupees with emotional stories, but donors almost never get to see a genuine hospital bill or medicine invoice afterward. We wanted to build a system where accountability isn't optional. In FundNexus, campaign creators cannot just withdraw donations whenever they want. Every withdrawal must be backed by real medical invoices or vendor bills, which an admin verifies before a single rupee leaves the account.</p>
      <p>During Phase 2, we built the core C++ backend, our custom linked list ledger, and SQL database tables. In this final Phase 3, we brought the entire application to life on the web. We built clean, separate dashboards for donors, fundraisers, and administrators. A donor can browse verified causes, donate through a simulated UPI or card checkout, and instantly get an 80G tax exemption receipt. Fundraisers can post progress updates and submit expense claims along with invoice pictures. Admins have an approval dashboard where they can inspect those bills and click approve or reject.</p>
      <p>We also connected our C++ engine to the Python web server using an IPC bridge so our linked list audit trail and copycat campaign detector run automatically behind every request. The whole platform is now completely functional, tested across our laptops and phones, and ready for our final project evaluation.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-title">Updated Project Approach and Architecture (2 pts)</div>
    <div class="instruction-sub">(Describe your current approach, including system design, communication protocols, libraries used, etc. Max 300 words).</div>
    <div class="content-box">
      <p>We designed FundNexus around three simple layers: a compiled C++ engine for fast algorithms, a Python backend server that handles API routes, and a responsive web frontend. We stuck with C++ for our core data structures because raw pointers and manual memory management give us much faster execution than pure JavaScript or Python, and we wanted zero memory leaks.</p>
      <p>Our C++ engine runs four key modules: a custom singly linked list that acts as our tamper-proof donation ledger, a FIFO queue that lines up pending expense claims in the exact order fundraisers upload them, a Jaccard token similarity check to catch copycat duplicate campaigns, and a rule-based risk scorer that flags unrealistic goals. Our Python backend runs using standard library HTTP modules to keep things lightweight. It talks directly to the compiled C++ executable (<code>engine.exe</code>) using standard input and output pipes (IPC), passing JSON data back and forth. For data storage, Divyanka set up a normalized 3NF SQLite database that keeps users, campaigns, donations, and expense receipts linked with foreign keys.</p>
      <p>To keep the math honest, our code strictly enforces one formula: <em>Remaining Balance = Total Verified Donations &minus; Admin-Approved Expenses</em>. If a fundraiser uploads a bill, the balance doesn't drop until the admin actually reviews and approves it. The frontend is built using clean HTML5, custom CSS, and vanilla JavaScript. It lets users switch between Guest, Donor, Fundraiser, and Admin views without needing heavy frameworks. Everything connects over standard REST API routes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="footer-row">
    <span>Page 3</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ==================== PAGE 4 ==================== -->
<div style="margin-bottom: 25px;">
